    {
      "id": "section-6a500fdfd285665c3850dac0",
      "kind": "content",
      "columns": [
        {
          "start": 1,
          "span": 12,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "html": "<h2>Primary research</h2><p>I focused on how users build trust, reduce friction, and make quick decisions. In daily life, most people rely on friends, delay tasks, or buy items they only need once.</p><p>A clear pattern emerged: users prefer access over ownership, but hesitate when the process feels uncertain or complicated. When borrowing takes too much effort, they default to buying.</p><p>The key insight is that users are not looking for borrowing platforms, they just want to solve a problem quickly and reliably. The experience needs to feel instant and intuitive, with clear trust signals and as few steps as possible, fitting naturally into existing habits.</p>"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
